{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1001306-gaming-at-a-distance-skype-et-all","id":1001306,"title":"Gaming at a Distance [Skype et all]","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness"],"posts":[{"date":"2016-11-10T04:38:19-08:00","id":1001306,"title":"Gaming at a Distance [Skype et all]","content":"I appear to have been elected to run a Mage the Awakening game in the not too distant. This will have to involve my actually picking one of the several ideas floating about in my brain to work from, but that\u2019s not the point.<br /> <br /> What is the point? Real life sucks. My gaming group is now scattered enough that getting into the same physical room on a weekly basis is no longer practical. People have jobs and responsibilities and animals and the prospect of children and other follies of the late 20s / early 30s that get in the way of gaming.<br /> <br /> This being the case, the concept of distance gaming by Skype or the like was floated. Given that this has certainly been done by forumites in the past, and given that the terms involved in searching for this are so broad as to produce useless results, I thought I would ask the assemblage for their experiences. Best software to use (Skype, Facebook chats, Discord etc.); whether video is useful or counterproductive, dice rolling suitable for CofD/nWoD, and other things that I haven\u2019t thought of but which are of critical import.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/528-darth-fanboy","name":"Darth Fanboy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar528_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"54"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-10T06:11:31-08:00","id":1001332,"title":"","content":"I suggest using Skype or Google Hangouts for the audio part, and using roll20 for dice. I've been playing by chat or skype for years, and I still do now. We're also playing D&amp;D Next while two players (one far away, the other even farther) join us in Google, while the rest of the group plays at my house, using roll20 for maps, tokens and dice rolls.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/316-jesuncolo","name":"Jesuncolo","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar316_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"493"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-10T07:14:22-08:00","id":1001345,"title":"","content":"My group use Roll20. Jesuncolo has a point about the audio quality being lower than Skype but it's never been enough of an issue for us to do anything about it.<br /> <br /> You don't need video but it can be helpful to make hand gestures. That may sound odd, but you do notice it if you don't have it.<br /> <br /> It's a bit different to normal RPing but mostly it just means you have to be a bit more considerate of other people.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://theonyxpath.com/downloads/Backer%20Badge%20small.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/178-michael","name":"Michael","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar178_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3560"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-10T11:37:29-08:00","id":1001412,"title":"","content":"Almost anything is fine if you're focusing on text and speech based stuff. There's actually some nice advantages that all the major options give you (you can type stuff to the group chat for logs to help with note-keeping, private message people with information, etc.).<br /> <br /> What I've noticed as the bigger determining factor is how much Virtual Table Top (VTT) you want. This is stuff like how easy it is to share multi-media (such as inspirational music, maps/diagrams of a room, etc.), how integrated gaming tools will be (dice rollers in chat or on a website, character sheets that are tracked online, etc.) and how interactive you want those things to be.<br /> <br /> If you're not worried about VTT functionality, any general program like Skype or Google Hangouts work fine. Video is useful if you like to sketch stuff and only need to show people for a few seconds, and it can be used if you want to do a physical map with tokens that you move and just keep the video on that. There are lots of dice-rollers out there both as websites or code you can put on a private space. You can use things like Google Drive to make communal spreadsheets in combat to track initiative, health, point expenditures, Tilts, and so on with out much hassle.<br /> <br /> If you want to get into more VTT stuff, which there's not as much CofD support as you might like, then you can narrow things down to more specialized RPG focused program options. There'll be more pre-loaded work on this route, but it can end it smoother stuff in play; esp. if you have more mechanically inclined players.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-10T12:00:56-08:00","id":1001416,"title":"","content":"I've personally had a lot of success with Google Hangouts, but that's primarily because I like recording most sessions for posterity. The Dice Roller app for Hangouts is rather simplistic, but it definitely works and is easy to use too. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":3436,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3436-dusksage","user":"Dusksage","date":".  Spellcheck 11-10-2016, 03:01 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3436-dusksage","name":"Dusksage","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3436_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"869"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-10T16:52:39-08:00","id":1001546,"title":"","content":"For virtual tabletop etc, I've never been a great one for maps and tactical combat, let alone when dealing with Mage. Sending the odd picture over whatever chat is in place for layouts or what have you should be more than sufficient. Keeping a life folder for documents / images, though, that was one that had not occurred.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/528-darth-fanboy","name":"Darth Fanboy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar528_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"54"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-11T01:04:46-08:00","id":1001667,"title":"","content":"I play in some incredibly non-standard games, so this might not be of any use to you. Sessions are usually 8-12 hours long for us, and there are usually several per week. Because everything happens through text, rapt attention isn't required - it plays like an extremely rapid PBP. Scenes take about 30 minutes to an hour to resolve on average.<br /> <br /> We use an IM client for the narrative. Which one doesn't matter incredibly much. One conversation for OOC, one for IC.<br /> We use <a href=\"http://rpgroller.com/nwod/\" target=\"_blank\">http://rpgroller.com/nwod/</a> for dice rolls. It has a dice pool limit of 20 (which has never mattered except in Mage, ymmv) and the Chance Die button is broken, but otherwise it works flawlessly.<br /> <br /> Our gaming style doesn't lend itself to any need for maps, so I can't offer any advise there.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3570-lnodiv","name":"lnodiv","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3570_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1607"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-11T09:59:28-08:00","id":1001821,"title":"","content":"For tabletops in general, I have found that using Google Drawings (the vidual art aspect of Google Docs) is a cheap amd relatively quick way to make maps, if one with a few quirks. You can use shapes to make and represent various things, wuch as doors, crevices and or alatrs by taking basic shapes and stacking them. New rooms are fairly easy to make, and if you set the background to a color and then overlay something with that same color as the body and outline with the same color, you can hide pre-assembled things away.<br /> <br /> Just remember to periodically put all character pogs/standins on them by right clicking them and selecting Bring To Top. New objects are always considered on the top layer, so your players may find the floor eating their characters out of nowhere.<br /> <br /> If nothing else, it is a good option for making quick maps to reference, and for letting players make their characters' living spaces.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5803-taidragon","name":"Taidragon","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5803_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1038"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-11T16:53:51-08:00","id":1001982,"title":"","content":"I have been running numerous groups and have 2 long running groups (2 years now) using Roll20. We use either Discord for Voice or Google For Voice/Vid. Roll20 has great additions such as scripting and such that make things really nice for a Storyteller. Also, there are the Character sheets and such and you can create your own as well if you are unhappy with them.. That or you can simply roll the dice and use a vid/voice app/site. I like Roll20 because I can create some nice backdrops and visions for everyone to see as the story unfolds around us. It works really nice.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;Kinda I want to&quot; - Trent Reznor<br /> <br /> <img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://scontent.ftpa1-2.fna.fbcdn.net/v/t1.0-9/27973518_2040009129348529_5319855605701568493_n.jpg?oh=9c85c5c9d4a0769a1249ea2f3e62ca43&amp;oe=5B002AA5\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12143-eldritchminister","name":"EldritchMinister","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12143_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"111"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}